text:
About this lecture
The cells of your body constantly send and receive signals. A signal is useless unless a cell has a receptor for it, and the receptor decides what happens next. This lecture gives the rules that every signalling pathway follows; the next lectures fill in the machinery.
After this lecture you can:

describe the signal types: endocrine, paracrine, autocrine, contact-dependent and synaptic;
name the receptor classes: ion-channel-coupled, G-protein-coupled, enzyme-coupled and intracellular (nuclear) receptors;
use Kd, dose–response curves, EC₅₀ and IC₅₀, and tell an agonist, a partial agonist and an antagonist apart;
explain amplification, integration, feedback and adaptation, and how molecular switches (phosphorylation, GTPases) work;
explain why some responses take seconds and others hours.

ELO 3 (cell signalling).

This lecture opens the block on cell signalling. We start with principles, the rules that hold for every pathway, and the following lectures look at the machinery in detail: G-protein-coupled receptors and second messengers, then enzyme-coupled receptors and kinase cascades, then the signals that shape tissues and decide cell fates. Today we use your chemistry a lot. Binding of a signal to a receptor is a chemical equilibrium, so the dissociation constant, K d, tells us how much signal is needed. From binding we go to the dose–response curve, which is the most important graph in pharmacology. You will learn the words a drug developer uses every day: agonist, antagonist, partial agonist, EC 50 and IC 50. Then we look at what the cell does with a signal after binding: it amplifies it, combines it with other signals, uses feedback, and adapts when the signal stays on. At the end we look at drugs that act on receptors, among them opioids, naloxone and beta-blockers. This covers ELO 3.

Why it matters: receptors are the main drug targets







β₂-adrenergic receptor (gold) with its G protein (green, blue, purple). Crystal structure, PDB 3SN6 (Kobilka lab, 2011).


About a third of all approved drugs act on GPCRs: beta-blockers, opioids, antihistamines, antipsychotics, GLP-1 drugs for diabetes.
The drug developer’s vocabulary:

affinity (Kd, Ki): how tightly it binds;
efficacy: how strongly it switches the receptor on (agonist, partial agonist, antagonist);
potency (EC₅₀, IC₅₀): how much drug is needed;
selectivity: which receptors it hits, and which it misses.

You meet these words again in Kliniska prövningar och läkemedelsutveckling.



Why should a future biomedical scientist care about all this? Because receptors are where most drugs act. About a third of all approved drugs act on G-protein-coupled receptors alone, and many more act on ion channels, enzyme-coupled receptors and nuclear receptors. The list includes beta-blockers for the heart, opioids for pain, antihistamines for allergy, many drugs for psychiatric disease, and the new GLP-1 receptor agonists such as semaglutide for diabetes and obesity. When a company develops a drug, it measures exactly the quantities you met today. Affinity is measured in binding assays, often as K i, the K d of an unlabelled drug determined by competition with a radioactive one. Efficacy and potency come from dose–response curves in cells. Selectivity compares the drug’s affinity for its target with its affinity for related receptors, because binding the wrong receptor causes side effects. The structure shows why GPCR drugs are possible at all: modern structures show the pocket where the drug binds, so new molecules can be designed to fit. You meet this vocabulary again in the course on clinical trials and drug development, Kliniska prövningar och läkemedelsutveckling.

About this lecture
A protein is not made once and kept forever. It must fold in a crowded cytosol, it is switched on and off by chemical marks, and sooner or later it is destroyed, some within minutes, some after years. Today: the life of a protein from the ribosome to the proteasome.
After this lecture you can:

explain why folding is risky in the cytosol and how chaperones (Hsp70, chaperonins) help;
explain how misfolded proteins aggregate and form amyloid;
describe how phosphorylation, acetylation and ubiquitination turn a protein into a switch;
describe the ubiquitin–proteasome system: E1, E2, E3, degrons and the proteasome;
use synthesis and degradation rates to explain protein half-life and steady state, and how they are measured (cycloheximide chase, pulse-chase);
explain how bortezomib, PROTACs and lenalidomide use this machinery, and how misfolding leads to Alzheimer’s and Parkinson’s disease.

ELO 4 (protein handling), with links to ELO 9 (disease).

In biochemistry you learned what a protein is: a chain of amino acids that folds into a shape, and the shape decides what it does. Today we follow a protein through its life inside a cell. It starts as a chain leaving the ribosome, and it must fold correctly in a very crowded space; helper proteins called chaperones make this possible. Once folded, a protein is often switched on and off by small chemical marks that enzymes add and remove. And finally every protein is destroyed, mostly by a machine called the proteasome, after being marked with a small protein called ubiquitin. The balance between making and destroying sets how much of each protein a cell has. We will also look at how we measure protein lifetimes, and at drugs that block or hijack the destruction machinery. Folding and quality control inside the endoplasmic reticulum, and degradation in lysosomes, get their own lectures; today we stay in the cytosol and nucleus. This lecture covers ELO 4, with links to disease mechanisms, ELO 9.

Degrons: what decides that a protein is destroyed?

A degron is a feature that an E3 recognises:

a phosphorylated motif (phospho-degron): the switch decides the protein’s life, as for IκB above, β-catenin and many cyclins and cell-cycle proteins;
exposed hydrophobic stretches of a misfolded protein (via Hsp70 and its partner E3s): quality control;
certain amino acids at the N-terminus (the N-degron rule).


E3 ligases do not pick substrates at random: they recognise degrons, short features that mark a protein as ready to destroy. The diagram shows a classic example, the transcription factor NF-kappa-B. In a resting cell, NF-kappa-B is held in the cytosol by an inhibitor protein, I-kappa-B. When the cell senses infection or inflammation, a kinase called IKK phosphorylates I-kappa-B on two serines. These two phosphates form a phospho-degron, which an E3 ligase recognises. The E3 adds a lysine-48 chain, and the proteasome destroys I-kappa-B within minutes. NF-kappa-B is now free, enters the nucleus and turns on genes for inflammation and cell survival. One of these genes encodes I-kappa-B itself, so the system switches itself off again: negative feedback. The same logic, phosphorylation followed by ubiquitination, controls beta-catenin in Wnt signalling and the destruction of cyclins in the cell cycle. A second type of degron is simply misfolding: exposed hydrophobic stretches are bound by chaperones, which recruit E3s. A third depends on which amino acid is at the very start of the chain, the N-degron rule.

About this lecture
The last lecture on the cytoskeleton. Actin and microtubules are tracks; today we meet the motor that pulls on actin, myosin, and the third filament system, the intermediate filaments, which makes cells strong.
After this lecture you can:

describe the myosin family and explain the cross-bridge cycle of myosin II: what ATP binding, ATP hydrolysis and phosphate release each do;
explain how a sarcomere shortens by sliding filaments, and why force depends on sarcomere length;
explain how Ca²⁺ switches contraction on through troponin and tropomyosin;
give examples of non-muscle myosin II (cell division, migration) and of myosin V as a cargo carrier;
describe how intermediate filaments are built, name the main families, and explain how they give mechanical strength;
choose a method to study the cytoskeleton: FRAP, TIRF, traction-force microscopy, optical tweezers;
explain how mavacamten treats hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and why broken keratin gives blistering skin.

ELO 6 (cytoskeleton and motility), with disease examples for ELO 9.

This lecture closes the cytoskeleton block. In the lecture on actin you saw how actin filaments grow and push the front of a migrating cell. In the lecture on microtubules you met kinesin and dynein, the motors that walk on microtubules. Today we add the motor of the actin system, myosin, and we see how one kind of myosin, myosin II, makes a muscle contract. We take the sarcomere apart, the repeating unit of muscle, and see how its geometry decides how much force a muscle can make. We see how calcium, the signal you met in the lecture on ion channels, switches contraction on. Then we turn to the third filament system, intermediate filaments, which do not move anything but stop cells and tissues from tearing. We end with a round-up of the methods used to study the whole cytoskeleton, and two medical stories: a new heart drug that acts directly on myosin, and a skin disease caused by broken keratin. The physiology of muscle and heart comes in the course on organ structure and function; here we stay at the level of molecules and cells.

About this lecture
Almost everything you will learn in this course about where things are in a cell was first seen in a microscope. Today is about how those pictures are made, and how far you can trust them.
After this lecture you can:

explain the difference between magnification and resolution, and why a light microscope cannot separate two objects closer than about 200 nm;
explain how phase contrast, DIC and fluorescence make transparent cells visible;
describe how we label one kind of protein: immunofluorescence (fixed cells) and fluorescent proteins such as GFP (living cells);
say what confocal, light-sheet, super-resolution and electron microscopy (TEM, SEM, cryo-EM, tomography) add;
choose a method for a question, and read a microscopy figure critically.

ELO 1 (cell and methods) and ELO 6 (methods to study the cytoskeleton).

This lecture follows the tour of the cell. In the tour we showed you organelles in pictures; today we ask how those pictures were made. Every microscope answers three questions in its own way. First, can it make small things big enough and sharp enough to separate them? That is resolution. Second, can it make a transparent cell stand out from the water around it? That is contrast. Third, can it show one particular molecule among thousands? That is specificity, or labelling. Keep these three words in mind; every method today improves one or more of them, and every method pays a price, usually that the cell must be dead, or that the picture is blurrier, or slower. A thread runs through the whole lecture: “How do we know?” A picture is evidence, and you need to know what a method can and cannot show before you believe it. This covers ELO 1, choosing a method, and supports ELO 6, because many of the examples are the cytoskeleton.

About this lecture
Every compartment in the cell is bounded by a membrane. Today: what a membrane is made of, why it forms by itself, why it is liquid, and how proteins sit in it. This is the base for the next lectures on transporters, pumps and ion channels.
After this lecture you can:

explain why lipids self-assemble into a bilayer (the hydrophobic effect), and name the main lipid classes;
explain what sets membrane fluidity (tail length, double bonds, cholesterol, temperature);
explain lipid asymmetry, and what flippases and scramblases do;
describe how membrane proteins are attached, and predict transmembrane helices from a hydropathy plot;
explain how FRAP, cell fusion and freeze-fracture EM showed that the membrane is a fluid with proteins in it;
explain how lipid nanoparticles and liposomes use these rules to deliver drugs.

ELO 2 (membranes and transport).

This is the first lecture of the membranes block. You met membranes in biochemistry: phospholipids, the bilayer, amphipathic molecules. We will not repeat that course. Instead we ask the cell biologist’s questions. Why does a bilayer form at all, and why does it seal itself? Why is it liquid, and how does a cell keep it at the right fluidity? Why are the two halves of the bilayer different, and what happens when that difference is lost? How do proteins sit in a membrane, and how can you predict that from a sequence? And how do we know any of this? At the end we look at medicines that are built from the same rules: the lipid nanoparticles in mRNA vaccines, and liposomes that carry a cancer drug. Everything today belongs to ELO 2. The next two lectures, on transport and on ion channels, use the membrane proteins we meet today.

About this lecture
Every cell has a voltage across its plasma membrane. Nerves and muscles use it to send signals, but so does every other cell: to move salt and water, to take up nutrients and to trigger secretion. Today: the proteins that make and use this voltage, the ion channels.
After this lecture you can:

explain how a channel differs from a transporter (rate, selectivity, gating);
explain why the K⁺ channel lets K⁺ through but not the smaller Na⁺;
describe the three main ways a channel opens: voltage, ligand, mechanical force;
derive the Nernst equation from ΔG and use it to calculate an equilibrium potential;
explain how the resting membrane potential arises, and what happens in an action potential;
explain how patch clamp records one channel, and how channel defects and channel drugs act (cystic fibrosis, local anaesthetics).

ELO 2 (membranes and transport), with a disease example for ELO 9.

This lecture builds on the two lectures before it. In the lecture on membrane structure you saw that a lipid bilayer is almost impermeable to ions. In the lecture on transporters and pumps you saw how the sodium-potassium pump uses ATP to build concentration gradients: high potassium inside, high sodium outside. Today we ask what happens when we open a hole for one kind of ion. The answer is a voltage, the membrane potential, and that voltage is used by every cell, not only nerves. We will use your chemistry: the Gibbs energy, delta G, which you know from equilibria, gives us the Nernst equation in three lines. We also meet one of the great methods of cell biology, patch clamp, which lets you watch a single protein molecule open and close in real time. We finish with two medical stories: cystic fibrosis, where modern drugs are matched to the exact molecular defect, and local anaesthetics, which you have probably met at the dentist. This covers ELO 2 and gives an example for ELO 9.

About this lecture
A hormone such as adrenaline never enters the cell. It binds a receptor on the outside, and within seconds the inside of the cell changes. Today: the largest family of receptors, the G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs), and the small second messengers they make inside the cell.
After this lecture you can:

describe a GPCR (seven transmembrane helices) and how a hormone switches it on;
explain the trimeric G protein as a timed switch, and the three main kinds: Gs, Gi, Gq;
follow the two main pathways: cAMP → PKA and IP₃ → Ca²⁺ / DAG → PKC, including calmodulin;
explain how the cascade amplifies the signal, and how every step is switched off (GTP hydrolysis, phosphodiesterase, GRK + arrestin, endocytosis);
describe how we watch second messengers in living cells (Ca²⁺ imaging, FRET sensors);
explain how cholera toxin, β-blockers, opioids and GLP-1 drugs act on this machinery.

ELO 3 (signalling), with drug and disease examples for ELO 9.

In the lecture on the principles of cell communication you met the receptor classes, dose–response curves and molecular switches. Today we open one receptor class in detail: the G-protein-coupled receptors. They are the largest family of membrane receptors in our genome, and they are the targets of about a third of all approved drugs, so this is the lecture where signalling meets pharmacology. We follow a signal from a hormone outside the cell, across the membrane, through a G protein, to a small molecule called a second messenger, and on to a protein kinase that changes what the cell does. We ask two quantitative questions: how can one hormone molecule outside cause millions of molecular events inside, and how does the cell switch the signal off again? We meet two methods that let us watch the signal live in a cell, and we finish with a bacterial toxin and three drug classes that act on exactly the steps we have drawn. This covers ELO 3, with examples for ELO 9.

About this lecture
The earlier signalling lectures followed fast signals: a hormone binds, a second messenger or a kinase cascade changes a protein within seconds. Today: signals that change which genes a cell uses, and so decide what the cell becomes. They build tissues in the embryo and keep them in shape in the adult.
After this lecture you can:

explain how a signal at the membrane ends as an active transcription factor and a gene-expression programme;
describe the core logic of Wnt/β-catenin, Hedgehog, Notch, TGF-β/SMAD, NF-κB and nuclear receptors, and how each is switched off;
explain lateral inhibition and morphogen gradients: how equal cells become different;
connect pathways to drugs and disease: tamoxifen, vismodegib, corticosteroids, APC mutations in colorectal cancer.

ELO 3 (signalling).

This lecture closes the signalling block. So far you have seen how a receptor talks to the inside of the cell: G proteins and second messengers, and kinase cascades. Those signals act fast, often in seconds, by changing the activity of proteins that already exist. Today the end point is different. The signals we meet now change gene expression, so the effect comes after hours and can last for days or for the rest of the cell’s life. That is how a cell in an embryo decides to become a neuron or a skin cell, and how adult tissues such as the gut lining keep the right number of each cell type. You already know gene regulation from earlier courses: transcription factors, enhancers, activators and repressors. We build on that. The question for every pathway is the same: how does a signal outside the cell switch on a transcription factor in the nucleus, and how is it switched off again? At the end we look at cancers and drugs that depend on exactly these steps.

About this lecture
The secretory pathway carries proteins out of the cell. Today we follow the opposite direction: how a cell takes things in from outside, sorts them, keeps what it can reuse, and digests the rest. The same digestion system also eats parts of the cell itself.
After this lecture you can:

describe clathrin-mediated endocytosis step by step, and the role of dynamin;
explain receptor-mediated uptake of LDL, and why receptors recycle while their cargo is digested;
explain how endosomes mature and acidify (V-ATPase), and how pH sorts cargo into recycling or degradation;
describe lysosomes, how their enzymes get there (mannose-6-phosphate), and the other routes in: macropinocytosis, phagocytosis, autophagy;
explain how statins, PCSK9 inhibitors, antibody–drug conjugates, lipid nanoparticles and enzyme replacement depend on this pathway.

ELO 4 (protein handling) and ELO 5 (organelles and homeostasis).

This lecture is the mirror image of the lecture on the Golgi and the secretory pathway. There, proteins made in the ER travelled outwards in vesicles to the cell surface. Here, material from outside the cell, and pieces of the plasma membrane itself, travel inwards. We will see that the cell does not simply swallow things at random. It uses receptors to pick out specific molecules, such as the cholesterol-carrying particle LDL, and it uses acid to sort them: the receptor goes back to the surface to be used again, and the cargo goes on to the lysosome, the digestive organelle of the cell. The lysosome also receives material from inside the cell by autophagy, so it is both the stomach and the recycling centre of the cell. Several important drugs depend on this pathway: cholesterol-lowering drugs, cancer drugs that are carried by antibodies, the lipid nanoparticles of mRNA vaccines, and enzymes given to patients with lysosomal storage diseases. The lecture covers ELO 4 and ELO 5.

About this lecture
A cell is not a bag of soup. Three kinds of protein filaments give it a shape, move things inside it and let it crawl. This lecture opens the cytoskeleton block with the overview and then the first system: actin filaments.
After this lecture you can:

compare actin filaments, microtubules and intermediate filaments;
explain how actin polymerises: G- and F-actin, ATP hydrolysis, the barbed (+) and pointed (−) ends, the critical concentration and treadmilling;
describe how nucleators (Arp2/3, formins) and actin-binding proteins (profilin, cofilin, capping protein, cross-linkers) control where and when filaments grow;
explain how lamellipodia, filopodia, stress fibres and the cortex are built, how Rho, Rac and Cdc42 switch them on, and how a cell crawls;
choose a method to see actin or block it (phalloidin, live imaging, drugs).

ELO 6 (cytoskeleton and motility).

This is the first of three lectures on the cytoskeleton. The word means “cell skeleton”, but a better picture is a set of roads and scaffolds that are built and torn down all the time. Three kinds of filaments make up the cytoskeleton: actin filaments, microtubules and intermediate filaments. Today we compare all three on one slide and then spend the rest of the lecture on actin. Actin is the filament that gives a cell its shape at the surface and lets it crawl. The key idea is that actin filaments are not fixed structures: subunits join and leave all the time, and the cell controls this with a small set of proteins and switches. Once you understand one filament, you can understand a crawling cell, a cancer cell invading tissue and a bacterium that hijacks the machinery. The next lecture covers microtubules and motor proteins; the one after covers myosin, muscle and intermediate filaments. This lecture covers ELO 6.

About this lecture
About a third of all human proteins are made into the endoplasmic reticulum (ER): every secreted protein, every protein of the plasma membrane, and the proteins of the ER, Golgi and lysosomes. Today: how they get in, how they fold, and how the ER checks them.
After this lecture you can:

explain how SRP and the translocon bring a growing chain into the ER, co-translationally;
predict the topology of a membrane protein from its signal sequence, stop-transfer and signal-anchor segments;
describe folding in the ER: BiP, disulfide bonds (PDI), N-glycosylation and the calnexin cycle;
explain ER quality control, ERAD and the unfolded protein response;
describe the smooth ER: lipids, drug metabolism and the Ca²⁺ store.

ELO 4 (protein handling) and ELO 5 (organelles and homeostasis).

In the lecture on protein sorting you met the idea that a protein carries a zip code that tells the cell where to send it. Today we follow the biggest route of all: into the endoplasmic reticulum, the ER. About one third of the proteins a human cell makes start their life here. That includes hormones like insulin, antibodies, receptors in the plasma membrane, transporters and channels, and the enzymes of the Golgi and the lysosomes. The ER does three jobs for these proteins. It brings them in across or into its membrane. It helps them fold, adds disulfide bonds and sugar chains. And it checks them, keeping and destroying the ones that fail. When too many fail, the ER sends an alarm, the unfolded protein response. At the end we look at the smooth ER, which makes lipids, breaks down drugs and stores calcium, and at two applications: diseases of misfolding, and antibodies made as drugs. This lecture serves ELO 4 and ELO 5.

About this lecture
A protein made on the rough ER still has a long way to go. Hormones, antibodies and digestive enzymes must leave the cell; receptors must reach the cell surface; lysosomal enzymes must reach the lysosomes. Today: the secretory pathway, the route from the ER through the Golgi apparatus to the cell surface, and the vesicles that carry proteins along it.
After this lecture you can:

describe the route ER → ERGIC → Golgi → trans-Golgi network → plasma membrane, and how a pulse-chase experiment shows it;
explain how a vesicle buds (coats COPII, COPI, clathrin; a small GTPase switch; cargo selection), finds its target (Rab GTPases, tethers) and fuses (SNAREs);
describe what the Golgi does to proteins (glycosylation, processing) and how cargo crosses the stack (cisternal maturation);
explain sorting in the trans-Golgi network, and constitutive versus regulated secretion;
explain how botulinum toxin and failing insulin secretion follow from this machinery.

ELO 4 (protein handling) and ELO 5 (organelles).

In the lecture on the endoplasmic reticulum you followed a new protein into the ER, saw it fold and saw how the ER checks its quality. Today we follow the proteins that pass that check. They leave the ER in small membrane bubbles called vesicles, pass through the Golgi apparatus, where their sugar chains are rebuilt, and are then sorted to the cell surface, to secretory granules or to the lysosomes. Along the way we meet a small set of machines that every transport step uses: coats that bend the membrane and choose the cargo, small GTPases that act as switches and address labels, and SNARE proteins that fuse two membranes. Most of the parts were found with three tools you will see today: radioactive pulse-chase, yeast genetics and a test-tube transport assay. We finish with two medical examples: botulinum toxin, which cuts SNAREs, and insulin secretion, which fails in type 2 diabetes. This lecture covers ELO 4 and ELO 5.

About this lecture
Growth factors, cytokines and insulin do not open channels or activate G proteins. Their receptors are enzymes, or bind enzymes: mostly tyrosine kinases. Today: how a receptor kinase passes a signal through a chain of kinases to the nucleus, and why these chains are the most common targets of modern cancer drugs.
After this lecture you can:

describe how a receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) is switched on: dimerisation, autophosphorylation, SH2 docking;
explain how Ras works as a GTPase switch, turned on by a GEF and off by a GAP;
follow three pathways: Ras–RAF–MEK–ERK, PI3K–Akt–mTOR and JAK–STAT;
explain how a kinase cascade amplifies a signal and makes a switch-like response, and how negative feedback and cross-talk shape it;
explain how phospho-specific antibodies show which kinase is active;
explain how imatinib, trastuzumab, EGFR and BRAF inhibitors act, and how tumours become resistant.

ELO 3 (signalling), with cancer examples for ELO 9.

This is the third lecture on cell signalling. In the lecture on principles of cell communication you met the receptor classes and the idea of molecular switches. In the lecture on G-protein-coupled receptors you followed a signal through a G protein to second messengers such as cyclic AMP and calcium. Today we look at the third big class of cell-surface receptors: enzyme-coupled receptors. Most of them are tyrosine kinases themselves, or they bind a tyrosine kinase. They answer growth factors, insulin and cytokines, so they control whether a cell divides, grows, survives or changes what it is. We will follow three pathways that you will meet again and again in later courses: the Ras–MAP kinase pathway, the PI3K–Akt pathway and the JAK–STAT pathway. We will also ask a systems question: why does a cell use a chain of three kinases instead of one? Finally, these pathways are where many cancers start, and where the first targeted cancer drugs act. This covers ELO 3, with examples for ELO 9.

About this lecture
A lipid bilayer keeps almost everything out. Yet a cell must take up sugars, amino acids and ions, throw out waste and drugs, and keep the inside very different from the outside. Today: the proteins that move molecules across membranes, and where the energy comes from.
After this lecture you can:

predict which molecules cross a pure lipid bilayer, and explain why most others need a protein;
explain passive and active transport with ΔG, and why transporters saturate like enzymes;
describe the alternating-access mechanism and uniport, symport and antiport;
explain primary active transport (P-type, V-type and ABC pumps; the Na⁺/K⁺ pump) and secondary active transport driven by the Na⁺ gradient;
explain how an epithelium moves glucose from the gut to the blood, and how drugs act on transporters.

ELO 2 (membranes and transport).

In the lecture on membrane structure you met the lipid bilayer: a thin, oily sheet that separates two water-filled spaces. Today we ask how anything gets through it. The answer has three parts. First, physics: a few small, uncharged molecules simply dissolve in the bilayer and cross on their own, but most of what a cell needs cannot. Second, proteins: transporters and channels give polar molecules and ions a path. Third, energy: to move something against its concentration gradient, the cell must pay, either directly with ATP or indirectly with another gradient. You already know the tools from chemistry and biochemistry: ΔG tells us which way a process goes, and Michaelis–Menten kinetics describes a protein with a limited number of binding sites. We will use both. Channels and the membrane potential get their own lecture next, so today channels appear only as a contrast to transporters. This lecture covers ELO 2, and it ends with drugs that act on transporters, which you meet again in drug development.

About this lecture
Microtubules are the cell’s long-distance tracks. They are built and torn down all the time, they point one way, and motor proteins walk along them carrying cargo. Today: how a microtubule is built, why it switches between growing and shrinking, how motors use ATP to walk, and what this means for neurons, cilia and cancer drugs.
After this lecture you can:

describe how αβ-tubulin builds a polar, hollow microtubule, and explain dynamic instability with the GTP cap (catastrophe and rescue);
explain how the centrosome and γ-tubulin start microtubules, and how the arrays differ in a fibroblast, a neuron and an epithelial cell;
compare kinesin and dynein (direction, ATP use, cargo), and explain why long cells need motors instead of diffusion;
describe the 9 + 2 axoneme of cilia and flagella and how dynein makes it bend;
give examples of methods (EB1 comets, single-molecule motor assays, optical traps), and explain how taxanes, vinca alkaloids and ciliopathies act on this machinery.

ELO 6 (cytoskeleton and motility).

This is the second lecture on the cytoskeleton. In the actin lecture you met the three filament systems side by side; today we zoom in on the one that is built for distance: the microtubule. We start with its building block, tubulin, and see how the way tubulin handles GTP makes each microtubule switch at random between growing and shrinking. That behaviour, called dynamic instability, lets a cell search its space and rebuild its tracks within minutes. Then we look at where microtubules start, the centrosome, and how different cell types arrange them. The second half is about the motors kinesin and dynein: how they walk, how we measured their steps one molecule at a time, and why a nerve cell one metre long would be impossible without them. We finish with cilia, which are microtubule machines that bend, and with two groups of cancer drugs and several diseases that act on this machinery. Everything today belongs to ELO 6.

About this lecture
The lectures before this one followed one organelle at a time: protein sorting, the ER, the Golgi, endosomes and lysosomes, protein turnover, mitochondria. Today we put the organelles side by side and ask how they work together.
After this lecture you can:

describe the nucleus and its envelope, including the nuclear lamina; peroxisomes; and lipid droplets;
explain what an organelle contact site is, and how organelles share lipids, Ca²⁺ and signals there;
explain homeostasis in each compartment as a set point kept by feedback: pH, Ca²⁺, energy and protein load;
compare all organelles in one table: membranes, DNA, how proteins get in, main job, pH and Ca²⁺;
explain two diseases of organelles that are not the usual suspects: progeria (lamin A) and Zellweger syndrome (no peroxisomes).

ELO 5 (organelles and homeostasis), with disease examples for ELO 9.

This lecture closes the block on protein handling and organelles. So far you have met each organelle on its own: how proteins are sorted, how the ER folds and checks them, how the Golgi modifies and ships them, how endosomes and lysosomes take material in and digest it, how proteins are made and destroyed, and how mitochondria make ATP. Today we do two new things. First, we fill the gaps: three organelles have not had their own lecture, namely the nucleus with its envelope and lamina, the peroxisome, and the lipid droplet. Second, we change the question. Instead of asking what one organelle does, we ask how all of them together keep the cell stable. That is homeostasis, and it is what ELO 5 asks for: compare organelles, and explain how they cooperate to keep energy, calcium, pH, lipids and protein load in balance. We end with a comparison table that you can use to revise for the exam, and with two diseases that show what happens when one organelle fails.

About this lecture
A human cell makes more than 10 000 different proteins, almost all of them on ribosomes in the cytosol. Each one must end up in the right place: the nucleus, the ER, a mitochondrion, a peroxisome, a lysosome, the plasma membrane or outside the cell. Today: how a protein knows where to go.
After this lecture you can:

explain how sorting signals in a protein’s sequence work like zip codes, and how we show that a signal is necessary and sufficient;
name the three ways in: gated (nuclear pores), transmembrane (ER, mitochondria, peroxisomes) and vesicular (from the ER onwards);
describe nuclear import and export: the NLS, importins, exportins and the Ran gradient;
describe import into mitochondria (TOM and TIM) and peroxisomes (PTS1);
predict where a protein with a given set of signals ends up, and explain how selinexor and NLS-tagged Cas9 use these rules.

ELO 4 (protein sorting and translocation) and ELO 5 (how organelles work together).

This lecture opens the block on protein handling and organelles. In biochemistry you learned how a ribosome makes a protein. Today we ask what happens next. A typical human cell makes more than ten thousand different proteins, and nearly all of them start on ribosomes in the cytosol. Yet each has a specific address. Histones must reach the nucleus, enzymes of the citric acid cycle must reach the mitochondrial matrix, insulin must leave the cell. The answer is that the address is written into the protein itself, as short stretches of amino acids called sorting signals. We will treat them as zip codes, and we will see the machines that read them. The plan: first the logic, using the topology rules from the cell tour; then the three kinds of transport, gated, transmembrane and vesicular; then the nucleus, mitochondria and peroxisomes in more detail. The ER, the Golgi and endocytosis get their own lectures, so we only set them up here. This covers ELO 4 and part of ELO 5.
